{
  "$schema": "https://turbo.build/schema.json",
  // EVERY task here is cache-less, and that is the point of the file. The goal is
  // ORDER and SELECTION, not speed: a cache hit is a green that did not execute,
  // which is the exact failure this repository has spent a long time removing.
  // Do not enable caching without a provably complete input set, and expect that
  // argument to be made in writing rather than by flipping this flag. `build` is
  // held to the same rule for the same reason: a cache hit on a build task is an
  // artefact nobody produced in this run, reported as if they had.
  //
  // `concurrency: "1"` is the other half of the point, and it belongs HERE rather
  // than in `TURBO_CONCURRENCY`: every test task in this workspace runs against ONE
  // pgvector database, provisioned once by `test/global-setup.ts` and seeded per
  // worker by `test/setup/test-database-url.ts`, so two suites must never write the
  // same schema in the same container. A limit carried by the graph cannot be
  // forgotten; an environment variable can.
  "concurrency": "1",
  // Turborepo 2 runs every task in STRICT env mode by default, so a task inherits
  // NOTHING from the invoking shell except what this file declares. That default is
  // safe for most tasks and wrong for exactly one here: CI supplies `DATABASE_URL`,
  // `LLMWIKI_TEST_DATABASE_URL` and `AKS_DATABASE_URL` in the `build-and-test` job's
  // `env:` block, and `//#test` is the suite that reads them. MEASURED without this
  // list: a probe task printed `LLMWIKI=UNSET DATABASE_URL=UNSET AKS=UNSET` and turbo
  // still reported `Tasks: 1 successful, 1 total`, exit 0 — a silent strip, not a
  // failure. The consequence in CI would be `test/fixtures/testcontainers-postgres.ts`
  // finding no override, provisioning its own container, and leaving the job's
  // `services: postgres` and both schema steps governing a database the suite never
  // opens.
  //
  // PASS-THROUGH, not `env`: `env` folds a name into the cache key, and caching is off
  // everywhere here on purpose; pass-through forwards without pretending the value is
  // part of the verdict. Chosen over `--env-mode=loose` because loose forwards EVERY
  // variable — the opposite of declaring what is needed — and a CLI flag would make
  // CI's graph differ from every local invocation. This list is the same declaration in
  // both places, and `test/control-gates.test.ts` fails closed if the job's env block
  // ever grows a key this list does not account for.
  "globalPassThroughEnv": ["DATABASE_URL", "LLMWIKI_TEST_DATABASE_URL", "AKS_DATABASE_URL"],
  "tasks": {
    // The ROOT project's build. Turbo names the root package `//`, never its
    // `package.json` name (`llm-wiki-compiler`), and `--filter=llm-wiki-compiler`
    // fails with `No package found with name 'llm-wiki-compiler' in workspace`. The
    // `//#` prefix is the ONLY way to register a root task —
    // `node_modules/turbo/docs/messages/missing-root-task-in-turbo-json.mdx:41-49` —
    // and there is no `--include-workspace-root` flag. Without this entry the root
    // `tsup` run that produces the distribution is absent from `turbo run build`
    // entirely, and the task exits 0 having built only `@repo/kg`.
    "//#build": {
      "dependsOn": [],
      "cache": false
    },
    // The ROOT project's test task: the full suite (`vitest run`), which is the gate
    // of record. It exists so CI can select it EXACTLY, with `--filter=//`, for the
    // reason the `test` comment below gives: unfiltered, this task plus
    // `@repo/kg#test` would run 549 of the same files twice.
    "//#test": {
      "dependsOn": [],
      "cache": false
    },
    // `build` is declared cache-less like every other task here, and it declares no
    // edge. It is the PER-PACKAGE half of the build surface: `@repo/kg#build` is the
    // only member task with a command. The distribution's build is `//#build`
    // above.
    "build": {
      "dependsOn": [],
      "cache": false
    },
    // NO `^test` EDGE, and none may be added back. The RULING this records:
    //
    // 1. Database serialisation is a CONCURRENCY concern, not a dependency edge.
    //    The single pgvector database is protected by `concurrency: "1"` above,
    //    which forces one task at a time across the whole run — strictly stronger
    //    than ordering along workspace edges, and it also covers siblings that do
    //    not depend on each other.
    // 2. A dependant's change does NOT invalidate its dependencies' tests. Those
    //    tests already passed on the tree they belong to, and re-running them
    //    because a dependent moved is work no filter asked for: with `^test`,
    //    `turbo run test --filter=@repo/wiki` executed 972 file-runs (406 of
    //    `@repo/foundation` + 566 of `@repo/wiki`) for a filter that selects 566.
    //
    // So a filtered run executes EXACTLY the filtered package's tests.
    "test": {
      "dependsOn": [],
      "cache": false
    },
    // Lint and typecheck read the tree and report. They order nothing, so they
    // declare no dependency edges at all.
    "lint": {
      "dependsOn": [],
      "cache": false
    },
    "typecheck": {
      "dependsOn": [],
      "cache": false
    },
    // `fallow` exists only for `@repo/kg`, the one package with a `.fallowrc.json`
    // and baselines. The other three declare no `fallow` script, so the task
    // resolves to nothing for them rather than to a scan over no baseline.
    "fallow": {
      "dependsOn": [],
      "cache": false
    }
  }
  // WHAT `turbo run build` EXECUTES, MEASURED ON TURBO 2.11.10, AND WHY THERE IS
  // STILL NO `^build` EDGE:
  // Unfiltered, `turbo run build` executes TWO real tasks, in two DISTINCT output
  // directories so neither can clobber the other:
  //   `//#build`      -> `tsup`                       -> `dist/`
  //   `@repo/kg#build` -> `tsc -p tsconfig.build.json` -> `packages/llmwiki-kg/ts/dist/`
  // It also RESOLVES three ids with no command — `@repo/api#build`,
  // `@repo/foundation#build`, `@repo/wiki#build` — which turbo marks `<NONEXISTENT>`
  // and drops from the run. Those three members emit nothing by design: they export
  // `./src/*.ts` and are bundled from source by the single root `tsup` run. A `build`
  // script there would report success without an artefact, which is the defect this
  // graph exists to avoid; `<NONEXISTENT>` is also why the task contributes nothing
  // for them rather than a false success.
  //
  // There is still no `^build` edge, and none is needed. The root `tsup` run does not
  // read `packages/llmwiki-kg/ts/dist`: it bundles every `@repo/*` package from
  // SOURCE, because those packages publish raw TypeScript paths (see the
  // `noExternal` comment in `tsup.config.ts`). Nothing in this graph consumes the
  // other build's output, so an ordering edge would order nothing — and in a
  // cache-less graph an edge is a claim about a dependency, not a scheduling hint.
  //
  // `@atomicstrata/llmwiki-core` used to be the second emitter and is collapsed
  // into `packages/wiki`, so it emits no artefact of its own.
}
